            "text": "{\n  \"verdict\": \"ACCEPTED\",\n  \"findings\": [],\n  \"missing_assumptions\": [],\n  \"counterexamples\": [],\n  \"limitations\": [\n    \"The result is a conditional mathematical counterexample, not a derivation of a native physical mechanism or a universal impossibility theorem.\",\n    \"The physical availability, ownership, preparation, and actual-event/channel association of the constructed operations (such as the E-flag or the retained-source effect Pi_p) remain unearned and are explicitly supplied as premises.\",\n    \"The construction relies on a specific finite-dimensional benchmark (4D source, 2D environment, 2D receiver) and does not rule out that other independent physical postulates or constraints might force receiver delivery.\"\n  ],\n  \"reconstruction\": [\n    \"General D_W completion: For any isometry W, D_W(X) = W^dagger X W + Tr[(I - W W^dagger) X] tau is CPTP and exactly recovers X from W X W^dagger. This demonstrates that individual global recoverability is a generic property of isometries, not a localization condition.\",\n    \"Common classical decoder: A single measure-and-prepare CPTP map D_cl, using a fixed basis partition (assigning |p,0,1\u003e and |p,1,0\u003e to u, and all other |j,e,r\u003e to j), recovers all diagonal source states for both W_R and W_E. It explicitly fails for the Bell representative's u atom, confirming it is not a universal quantum decoder.\",\n    \"Common quantum decoder: Two isometries A and B (where B is the old W_E) are constructed to implement the same reduced source channel C. Linear maps L_0 and L_1 are defined such that L_0 B = I, L_1 B = 0, L_0 A = I/sqrt(2), and L_1 A = I/sqrt(2). The CPTP completion D(X) = L_0 X L_0^dagger + L_1 X L_1^dagger + Tr[(I-P)X]|o\u003e\u003co| exactly recovers all quantum states for both A and B.\",\n    \"R blindness vs contrast: Under B, the receiver R is always in the state |0\u003e\u003c0| (completely blind). Under A, R is |+\u003e\u003c+| for ret